    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0211",
      "sequence": 223,
      "meaning": "At the touch of his holy, grief-destroying feet, the true mass of austerity appeared. Seeing Raghunātha, giver of joy to his people, Ahalyā stood before him with joined hands. Overwhelmed by love, body thrilled and speechless, she fell at his feet in supreme good fortune while streams flowed from both eyes. Steadying her mind, she recognized the Lord and by Raghupati’s grace received devotion. In utterly pure speech she began a hymn: ‘Victory to Raghurāya, accessible through knowledge. I am an impure woman; you are the purifier of the world, Rāvaṇa’s foe and giver of joy. Lotus-eyed releaser from worldly fear, protect me—I have come for refuge. The sage’s curse was very good; I count it highest grace, for I have looked upon Hari, liberator from becoming, the gain Śaṅkara knows. My mind is simple and I ask no other boon: let my bee-like mind drink loving nectar from the pollen of your lotus feet. From those feet the supremely holy Gaṅgā appeared and Śiva bore her on his head; Brahmā worships those same lotuses, and merciful Hari has placed them on my head.’ Again and again Gautama’s wife fell at Hari’s feet, then departed; receiving the boon dearest to her heart, she went joyfully to her husband’s world.",
